@model ReformedHistory.Views.Content.Confessions.Secret.SecretSection

<h2 id="@this.Model.Link">Chapter @this.Model.Index</h2>
<h3>@this.Model.Name</h3>
<br />

<div class="centerQuote">
    <span>
        There is one covenant and one church of all the
        saints before and after Christ, one way to heaven, and one
        unchanging religion of all the saints.
    </span>
    <span>Heinrich Bullinger, The One and Eternal Testament or Covenant of God</span>
</div>
    
<ol>
    <li>
        Since the beginning of the world, God has made one holy and eternal covenant with our forefathers
        in the faith, that He would be their God and they would be His people.
    </li>
    <li>
        God's covenant is one of grace alone, for none can merit the favor and mercy of God through his own work.
    </li>
    <li>
        The promises of the covenant have their ultimate fulfilment in Jesus, who saves His people from their sins 
        and adopts all who believe in Him as sons of God so that they may have eternal life. All the saints who 
        are saved, or who were ever saved, through God's covenant are saved through Jesus Christ.
    </li>
    <li>
        Just as God promises to be our God, the covenant requires that we also promise to be his people. 
        Faithlessness, disobedience, and rebellion break the covenant and alienate man from God.
    </li>
    <li>
        Though the covenant is one, throughout the ages there have been different administrations under
        Adam, Noah, Abraham, Isacc, Jacob, Moses, David, and now finally Jesus. A common name for
        the administration of Moses is the old covenant, and a name for the administration of
        Jesus is the new covenant.
    </li>
    <li>
        The ordinances, sacraments, and practices of the covenant may change in different administrations, but
        the covenant promises remain eternal.
    </li>
</ol>
<br />